      "Language": "jpn",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "A 49 year-old-man with known situs inversus totalis was admitted to our hospital with a history of angina pectoris of 4 months duration. Coronary arteriography revealed that the left main trunk had a 90% stenosis. After the arteriography, the patient's hemodynamics was deteriorated, and he required a support of intraaortic balloon pumping. He underwent emergent coronary artery bypass grafting with saphenous veins the following day, and recovered uneventfully. Technical problems in this patient from a surgical viewpoint were discussed. As far as we know, this is the first case of coronary bypass grafting on a patient with mirror-image dextrocardia and a stenosis of the left main trunk."},
